Bootstrap: docker
From: pytorch/pytorch:2.3.1-cuda12.1-cudnn8-runtime

# PLACER -- stochastic active-site repacking, for preorganisation.
#
# This image is an ENVIRONMENT, not an application. PLACER's own checkout and
# weights live on FAC under $CHPC_LAB/data/placer and are named by PLACER_DIR,
# because the job invokes run_PLACER.py by path and cd's into the checkout.
# Keeping the code outside means a PLACER update is a git pull, not a rebuild
# of a 9 GB image.
#
# The version pins are not preference, they are PLACER's environment lockfile
# (envs/placer_env.yml): python 3.10, torch 2.3.1 cu121, dgl 2.4.0 th23 cu121,
# e3nn 0.5.4. DGL is what freezes it -- DGL publishes no wheels past torch 2.4,
# so "just bump torch" is not available, and the SE3Transformer PLACER imports
# is a DGL program.
#
# HARDWARE LIMIT: cu121 has no sm_120 kernels, so this cannot run on the RTX
# Pro 6000 (Blackwell) nodes ggpu1-[13-19]. Fine on L40S (sm_89) and H100/H200
# (sm_90). Jobs must say --gres=gpu:nvidia_l40s:1 rather than --gres=gpu:1,
# which can land anywhere and dies with "no kernel image is available for
# execution on the device".
#
# Build:  chpc-tool build placer

%labels
    Tool         placer
    Version      1.0
    Upstream     https://github.com/baker-laboratory/PLACER
    UpstreamRef  7dc5563300e7143582d85636bdd413d4c7688e9a
    Licence      BSD-3-Clause (covers source AND model weights)
    Base         pytorch/pytorch:2.3.1-cuda12.1-cudnn8-runtime
    GPUArch      sm_89 sm_90 only -- NOT sm_120 (dgl/cu121)
    Maintainer   Craik Lab, UCSF

%post
    set -eux
    export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
    apt-get update
    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends git ca-certificates
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

    python -c "import sys; assert sys.version_info[:2] == (3,10), sys.version"

    # PEP 668: the pytorch base images ship a conda python marked
    # EXTERNALLY-MANAGED, so a bare `pip install` refuses with
    #   error: externally-managed-environment
    # and apptainer then reports "exit status 1" under a message suggesting
    # --ignore-fakeroot-command, which has nothing to do with it. Inside a
    # container the system interpreter IS the environment -- there is no OS
    # package manager to conflict with and nothing else sharing it -- so
    # overriding the marker is correct here, not a workaround.
    export PIP_BREAK_SYSTEM_PACKAGES=1

    # DGL is not on PyPI for CUDA builds; it has its own per-torch wheel index.
    pip install --no-cache-dir dgl==2.4.0 -f https://data.dgl.ai/wheels/torch-2.3/cu121/repo.html
    pip install --no-cache-dir e3nn==0.5.4
    pip install --no-cache-dir "se3-transformer @ git+https://github.com/NVIDIA/DeepLearningExamples.git#subdirectory=DGLPyTorch/DrugDiscovery/SE3Transformer"

    # openbabel has no usable PyPI wheel (it is a C++ library with python
    # bindings); conda-forge is the supported route and this base is a conda
    # image, so use it rather than fighting a source build.
    #
    # libmamba, not the classic solver. This is the single heaviest step in
    # any recipe here, and the login node's user slice has a SOFT memory
    # limit of 8 GiB under its 16 GiB hard one -- between the two the kernel
    # throttles and reclaims rather than killing, so a classic conda solve
    # against conda-forge does not fail, it just crawls for tens of minutes
    # and looks like a hang. The C++ solver is a fraction of the footprint
    # and is the default in modern conda anyway; naming it means the build
    # does not depend on which conda the base image happens to ship.
    conda install -y -n base -c conda-forge conda-libmamba-solver || true
    conda install -y --solver=libmamba -c conda-forge openbabel=3.1.1 \
        || conda install -y -c conda-forge openbabel=3.1.1
    conda clean -afy

    # Validate the environment against the real code. The checkout does not
    # ship in this image, so clone it, import through it, and throw it away --
    # otherwise the first thing to discover a broken dgl/e3nn/torch triple
    # would be a six-hour array job.
    git clone https://github.com/baker-laboratory/PLACER /tmp/PLACER
    git -C /tmp/PLACER checkout --detach 7dc5563300e7143582d85636bdd413d4c7688e9a
    cd /tmp/PLACER
    python -c "
import torch, dgl, e3nn
print('torch', torch.__version__, 'dgl', dgl.__version__, 'e3nn', e3nn.__version__)
from openbabel import openbabel
import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/tmp/PLACER')
import PLACER
print('PLACER imports')
"
    test -s /tmp/PLACER/weights/PLACER_model_1.pt
    cd /
    rm -rf /tmp/PLACER

%environment
    export LC_ALL=C.UTF-8
    export PYTHONNOUSERSITE=1
    # PLACER_DIR is set by the registry, not here: the checkout is on the FAC
    # share and its path is a site fact.

%test
    set -eu
    python -c "import torch, dgl, e3nn; from openbabel import openbabel; print('env ok', torch.__version__, dgl.__version__)"

%runscript
    exec "$@"

%help
    PLACER's environment. The code and weights are on FAC:

      chpc-tool exec placer -- python "$PLACER_DIR/run_PLACER.py" --help

    Install the checkout once (login node):

      git clone https://github.com/baker-laboratory/PLACER "$CHPC_LAB/data/placer"
      git -C "$CHPC_LAB/data/placer" checkout --detach 7dc5563

    Two things this pipeline learned the hard way:
      - PLACER's multi-model output MUST be split before scoring. Handing the
        whole ensemble to a scorer that reads model 0 scores sample 1 n times
        and reports a fraction of exactly 0.000 or 1.000.
      - Preorganisation measured off a constrained relax is not a measurement;
        the constraints impose the geometry. PLACER's stochastic sampling is
        what makes that column honest.

    Needs --gres=gpu:nvidia_l40s:1 (or H100/H200), never a bare --gres=gpu:1.
